\description{
Orchestrates multiple sequence alignment (MSA) and automated quality-control masking across orthologous sequence clusters.
Primary alignment hypotheses are inferred using \code{MAFFT} (Katoh & Standley, 2013). Subsequently, ambiguous sites,
poorly aligned terminal fragments, and non-homologous insertions are masked using the \code{DECIPHER} framework (Wright, 2024),
eliminating systematic noise while retaining phylogenetically informative nucleotide positions for downstream supermatrix assembly.
}
\details{
\strong{Masking policy.} With \code{mask_alignment_regions = TRUE} (the default) the \code{MAFFT} alignment is
passed through \code{DECIPHER::RemoveGaps()} and \code{DECIPHER::MaskAlignment()}, and the resulting
post-masking column set is what \code{min_non_gap_fraction}, \code{max_missing_fraction} and
\code{min_masked_alignment_length} are evaluated against. With \code{mask_alignment_regions = FALSE} the
\code{MAFFT} alignment is exported unmodified and \strong{those three thresholds are not applied at this
stage}: they are relative to a masked column set that does not exist in that branch. Occupancy
filtering then happens once, on the joint ingroup plus outgroup alignment, in
\code{run_joint_realignment()}. This is the recommended setting for a sparsely sampled outgroup:
masking two to seven highly divergent accessions on their own defines a column set the ingroup
does not share and can erode an outgroup alignment to a few base pairs, which then fails the
occupancy filter of the joint realignment and removes the outgroup precisely from the loci
needed to root the tree. Both the chosen policy and the parameter values are written to
\code{logs/LOG_alignment_run_info.txt} and stamped into every per-marker summary table.

\strong{Caching.} A marker is reused from a previous run only when its exported alignment, summary
table and filter log all exist \emph{and} the five parameters stamped into the cached summary table
match the current call. Any parameter change invalidates the cache and the marker is
reprocessed, so changing \code{mask_alignment_regions} on an already populated output directory
takes effect instead of silently returning the previous alignments.

\strong{Ambiguity policy.} IUPAC ambiguity codes are retained by default (\code{preserve_iupac = TRUE}).
The downstream tools all accept them, and they carry information that \code{N} does not: an \code{R}
site constrains the state to A or G, an \code{N} constrains nothing. There is no analytical reason
to discard that constraint, so the pipeline does not.

Ambiguity is nevertheless measured rather than assumed away. The marker summary reports
\verb{mean_fraction_ambiguous_*} for each of the five processing stages, and \verb{n_sites_ambiguous_*}
for the raw input and the final alignment. The \code{raw_input} figures are computed before
\code{clean_ambiguous()} is applied, so they record what the source records actually contained
regardless of the policy in force. That is what makes a per-locus decision possible: a marker
whose ambiguity is concentrated rather than diffuse can be examined on its own evidence
instead of being subjected to a global rule.
